{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"INVESTIGATED BY THE OWNING SESSION, AS THIS TICKET ASKED. The observation was real and correctly\nreported; the candidate root cause it offered is WRONG, and the actual cause is now fixed and proven.\n\nThank you for filing it rather than investigating — the \"candidate, not a finding\" framing was exactly\nright, and the observation is what led here.\n\n=== THE OBSERVATION IS REAL. THE STATED CAUSE IS NOT. ===\n\nReported: \"DC inbound filing is a no-op ... no local ticket created from DC issue\". The actual defect\nwas in the TEST'S ASSERTION, not in inbound filing. Filed and fixed as\n[rebar:23ed-de0a-765b-49aa], merged as change 1124.\n\nThe assertion could never have passed, for two independent reasons:\n\n  * `_jira_key_to_local_id` LOWERCASES (`inbound_translate.py:120-124`): `RBJNAZV-1` becomes\n    `jira-rbjnazv-1`. The test's `remote_key in json.dumps(t)` check is case-sensitive.\n  * the inbound CREATE payload (`apply_inbound_records.py:183-212`) carries NO raw Jira key at all —\n    the key appears only as a TITLE FALLBACK, unused whenever the issue has a summary, as it does\n    here (\"J7 provenance oracle\").\n\nVerified against the exact payload shape the inbound path writes: `remote_key in json.dumps(ticket)`\nis False. So a PERFECTLY-CREATED ticket produced a red, and a genuinely-missing one would have too —\nthe oracle could not distinguish them in either direction.\n\nAlso worth correcting for the record: `filed=0` in that stdout is NOT the count of tickets filed from\nDC issues. `filed` counts INVARIANT-VIOLATION bug tickets (`run_differs.py:282`,\n`check_at_most_one_local_id`), so `filed=0` is healthy and says nothing about inbound. `scanned=1`\nand `OK: applied 1 of 1 mutations` were, in fact, consistent with a correct inbound create all along.\n\n=== PROOF THAT INBOUND FILING WORKS ===\n\nGitHub Actions run **30660546548**, job \"jira DC harness smoke\", against main @ fde6baae7 (the first\nmain containing the fixed oracle):\n\n    [jira-live-canary] collected=17 executed=17 skipped=0\n    1 failed, 16 passed in 13.55s\n\n`test_a_dc_created_ticket_carries_the_shared_jira_provenance` **PASSED**. It now asserts:\n  * a local ticket exists whose id EQUALS `_jira_key_to_local_id(remote_key)` — exact match on the\n    derived id, not a substring scan of a JSON blob;\n  * the BINDING STORE maps that local id to the DC key, so the ticket corresponds to THAT issue;\n  * `creation_channel == \"jira\"`.\n\n`executed=17 skipped=0` matters as much as the pass count: nothing silently skipped.\n\nThe single remaining failure in that run was\n`test_the_documented_setup_refuses_to_read_the_pat_from_config`, an unrelated credential defect filed\nas [rebar:cd78-d877-3d25-4ec3] and since merged.\n\n=== WHY THIS WAS NEWLY VISIBLE, WHICH THIS TICKET GOT RIGHT ===\n\nYour reasoning that bug 8070's fix un-masked the tier is correct, and it is worth recording that\nTHREE defects were masking this assertion in sequence, each hiding the next:\n  1. an EMPTY store made the pass report `scanned=0` and \"converged\" — indistinguishable from working;\n  2. the PAT-cap bug [rebar:f391-806b-938f-45d8] killed the suite at SETUP before the test ran;\n  3. the oracle itself could not pass (23ed).\nOnly with all three fixed does this test's verdict carry information.\n\nDISPOSITION: closing as a duplicate of [rebar:23ed-de0a-765b-49aa], which is the same underlying issue\ndiagnosed to its actual cause and already fixed, merged and closed. No inbound-filing defect exists."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785529749817352001,"uuid":"705f61db-3c77-48b3-b88d-399bdad625df"}